{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting","id":1450550,"title":"How to make/keep Werewolf combat dangerous and interesting?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-01T01:15:50-07:00","id":1450550,"title":"How to make/keep Werewolf combat dangerous and interesting?","content":"Disclaimer: I don't have much experience with Werewolf combat, or Chronicles combat in general (my chronicles are more focused on investigations and mysteries, rather than on violence). Reading Werewolf mechanics makes me think that basically there are three &quot;tiers&quot; of werewolf combat:<br /> 1) Not using Gauru;<br /> 2) Using Gauru without falling in Kuruth;<br /> 3) Falling in Kuruth.<br /> <br /> By &quot;tiers&quot; I mean that those combat situations are pretty distinct. To kill werewolf in Gauru (and I state as an axiom that for combat to be interesting for players, there should be real possibility of being killed in combat) you have to be able to deal at least Health in lethal damage per turn while overcoming Defence and possible armor, or have source of aggravated damage. My problem is that this amount of damage being dealt practically forbids usage of non-Gauru forms in this particular combat. For example, let's imagine our theoretical pack fighting the umia of fire spirits with potent Blast numina. If they deal \u00b110 level of lethal damage, that's basically acceptable for pack Rahu with 10 levels of health, going to Gauru and then straight to Kuruth. But everyone else dies on spot if fire spirits concentrate on him, so they <i>need</i> to use Gauru as well.<br /> <br /> This divides combat in those three tiers I explained above. Which is manageable if you try to pit combat in places where you can't go into Kuruth as often as possible. My problem is that, for example, in Shadow you don't have strong incentive not to go into Kuruth, ripping and tearing your way through the enemies (or I don't see it). So certain locations actively prohibit battling in normal human/wolf form. Other problem is that tiers basically enforce certain way to &quot;solve&quot; the combat. If you have a brood of Azlu, you either make them strong enough so pack <i>has</i> to go into Kuruth, or you don't (and they just die anyways if your pack choose to do this). You don't have any middle ground - or again, I don't see it from my lack of experience with this line.<br /> <br /> Do you encounter the same issues? How do you solve them? Is there something I don't see about this? I actively want to do interesting combat scenes in Werewolf, but solving them through flipping the switch and going into Gauru is not interesting - and never allowing Gauru and Kuruth by always forcing PCs to fight in unfavorable conditions is not very viable - and again, not very interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T08:14:42-07:00","id":1450587,"title":"","content":"I am so glad you started this thread. I find that Werewolf Second edition has a great deal of challenges in combat from a purely mathematical point of view. Here are my thoughts on the matter. <br /> <br /> 1) Not using Gauru; This one is the simplest. Out of Garou a werewolf is simply a human, plus regeneration plus gifts. However, they would be relatively flimsy. At this point they do not have crazy regeneration so there is no need to &quot;roll over damage&quot;. One option is to ambush a werewolf with firearms while they are in human form and fill the whole healthtrack with lethal or aggravated damage (not too difficult using and ambush and firearms)<br /> <br /> <br /> 2) Using Gauru without falling in Kuruth; This is probably the default. A werewolf in Garou can probably destroy one of the enemies in the core book, I use the Claimed hound as a challenging foe, but still kind of Cannon Fodder. Using the Garou form can probably demolish one, but after using the Garou form and probably expending Willpower and Essence, if there are any other enemies that might be a challenge. Alternatively, the foe can dodge a werewolf in garou which would be a headache, who would be crazy enough to stand and fight?<br /> <br /> 3) Falling in Kuruth. This is the big Elephant in the room. One could dogpile the Raging Werewolf and roll over damage, but you probably need lots of guys to do that. A swarm of Hosts, a bunch of spirits, a pack of claimed, a squad of humans with guns are probably needed to bring it down (especially since humans are probably affected by lunacy). <br /> <br /> ..or, you play is smart and use Silver. That is the great equalizer. Get some claimed with improvised pieces of silver, load up shotguns with silver buckshot (look online, they actually sell silver shot for industrial purposes, really), forge a silver knife and the fight becomes much easier. <br /> <br /> Alternatively, you can run away. I think that since the Garou will collapse after the Garou is finished, one can move in for the kill afterwards. Climb walls, run away if your speed is higher, flee to twilight. I figure that a claimed might have his host body slaughtered, but the spirit can possess a corpse and move in for the kill after the Kuruth ends (and what Kuruth does not have an abundance of corpses).<br /> <br /> or...you can look at the material damage of Kuruth. Did the Werewolf kill a packmate, or destroy some property, or engage in cannibalism (a big no-no according to the oath of the moon). Is the territory filled with fear? The werewolf might be alive but the damage done might be irrepairable. <br /> <br /> There are lots of rich possibilities!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T17:43:15-07:00","id":1450653,"title":"","content":"Having been playing Werewolf second edition for some time I don't find this to be a big problem. The question you have to ask yourself is this: what role do you want combat to play in your game?<br /> <br /> In most RPGs combat is a challenge - a difficult task that the characters have to work together to overcome. My contention is that, except for a few set pieces, <i>combat itself should rarely be the challenge in Werewolf</i>.<br /> <br /> Werewolves are, to be frank, supposed to be ridiculously overpowered in comparison to most of the threats they will encounter from one night to the next. The other important thing to note is that <i>most of the foes Werewolves face know this</i>. The <i>Azlu </i>or Spirit <i>umia</i> you want to set up as antagonists for the pack aren't going to be stupid enough to take them on in a head-on fight. That's a good way to get yourself Gauru'd.<br /> <br /> For Werewolves then, the main challenge of the game is in <i>finding</i> the prey that's causing them issues and dealing with problems you <i>can't</i> solve by simply going Gauru. To take your example, perhaps the fire spirits might be hiding out in the local incinerator that's responsible for disposing of municipal waste. It's operated by the government and guarded 24/7 so the Werewolves can't just waltz in and go Gauru. In this case, the challenge the pack faces is first in locating the fire spirits, and second in setting the stage such that they can gain access to the building and take the spirits out without too many repercussions.<br /> <br /> Which brings me to the second major point. If you think about the themes of Werewolf, one that it focuses on is the nature of wolves (and to a certain extent humans) as hunters. If you think about hunting animals in nature, it's typically identifying, stalking, and chasing prey that's the hard part of the hunt. Actually killing the prey usually isn't the difficult part, and in almost all cases the failure state for a hunt is that the prey <i>escapes</i>, not that the prey turns around and murders you. Actually making the kill (and getting to eat) is the <i>reward</i> of the hunt, not the challenge.<br /> <br /> For players then, getting the chance to use all their cool Gauru powers and feel like a badass Werewolf is the reward for a well-executed hunt. All the other parts of the hunt can and often should be difficult - just finding the prey, chasing it down, etc. Most prey will only fight Werewolves if it has no other options, and the job of Werewolves is to arrange things so that that is the case.<br /> <div align=\"center\"> <hr /></div> <br /> So, with that all out of the way, let's now consider big set piece fights, and how to make combat interesting for Werewolves.<br /> <br /> Fights in CofD are nearly always short - from experience even two full packs of Uratha in Gauru will kill each other in 4-5 turns. As a result, you can consider death rage as lasting &quot;forever&quot; in a combat, since even the lowest Primal Urge Uratha get 10 minutes of it (100 turns if you take each round as being 6 seconds).<br /> <br /> So if the Werewolves death rage their health regeneration becomes essentially infinite. However, the players should <i>not </i>see death rage as a positive state for their characters. The usual way to counteract this is to make death rage result in the Werewolves destroying something and killing innocent bystanders. But let\u2019s be frank for a moment, most players even if they're committed to roleplaying, don't actually give two hoots that they smashed up the local library and ate the librarian. In fact, they probably got all their essence back from eating a human and so on balance they might see it as a good thing.<br /> <br /> A better way to balance death rage is to <i>make it important that the player keeps control of their character</i>. There are lots of ways you can do this, but the easiest is to set things up so that the fire they're putting out right now isn't the only fire they have to put out tonight. Ten plus minutes of death rage, the resulting destruction, and then calls to emergency services, police, roadblocks etc. could be catastrophic if the characters are on a tight schedule, even if they don't get caught.<br /> <br /> If the players can't (and don't want to) Death Rage, their relationship with Gauru also changes. Gauru can only be taken once per scene, and only maintained for so many turns. It also usually takes either an action or an essence to shift. With these restriction in mind, Gauru then becomes a resource that they can deploy tactically for a few turns - probably after they've taken a fair amount of lethal and need to heal themselves back up. And for players who didn't build combat focused characters, Gauru may be their only way to contribute meaningfully to the fight. For those players, taking Gauru means choosing the moment when they can have the greatest impact and deploying the form then.<br /> <br /> Having the pack need to run from one fire to another also lets you wear the characters down without needing to touch their health boxes. A Werewolf without essence or willpower is actually a lot less threatening than you might think, and barring oath violations, these resources come back slowly. If you have the pack face off against multiple sets of foes in a single night, even if those foes aren't a threat in and of themselves, the repeated drain on their resources might turn them into a more substantial threat. <div align=\"center\"> <hr /></div> <br /> Putting these things together we can imagine a situation where the pack has tracked a claimed to the place where it's hiding out with the cult it has been building up. Since the pack failed a subterfuge roll last session, the claimed knows that the pack is on to it, and has planned for this moment. When the pack kick down the door, the claimed is already getting into a car in the basement garage. Meanwhile, its cult closes in around the pack and opens fire with handguns plus a couple of rifles and one submachine gun.<br /> <br /> In this situation the pack needs to chase after the claimed before it gets too far away. Killing these humans is not a problem - the challenge for the pack here is how to deal with these humans <i>quickly</i> and <i>without using too many resources</i>. They're going to need those resources later when they are chasing down and eventually fighting the claimed. Death raging here is not an option either - they can't afford to waste ten minutes or more killing these humans (not to mention the fact that they would almost certainly break the Oath of the Moon and eat one of these brainwashed people) because by then the claimed will be in another part of the city or in some other bolthole.<br /> <br /> Resolving this problem could be a great chance for one of the less frontline characters to shine. Perhaps the Elodoth has a gift that can pacify the cultists, or the Cahalith can impose a specific lunacy condition on them all that will allow the pack to escape quickly. How long it takes to get away will determine how far behind they are in the chase scene that comes immediately afterwards, and whether they win or lose that chase will determine if the claimed has the opportunity to arm a homemade explosive that he's got on the door of his safehouse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2384,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","user":"Aurumae","date":". 06-01-2021, 08:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T19:18:15-07:00","id":1450657,"title":"","content":"Thanks, that was a very well written post","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T07:50:53-07:00","id":1450684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450653#post1450653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having been playing Werewolf second edition for some time I don't find this to be a big problem. The question you have to ask yourself is this: what role do you want combat to play in your game?<br /> <br /> In most RPGs combat is a challenge - a difficult task that the characters have to work together to overcome. My contention is that, except for a few set pieces, <i>combat itself should rarely be the challenge in Werewolf</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the problem I have. For part which is rarely the challenge, there is too much content devoted to this part. Frankly, if combat is not meant to be a challenge, then entire Auspice has nothing to do for a living (looking at you, Rahu), and neither does tremendous amount of Gifts and Merits and what-have-you. If combat is not meant to be a tactical challenge for the players, there is next to zero reason to have anything but Down-and-Dirty rules for combat, or even a sidebar with &quot;werewolves win&quot;. For me, combat has meaning if and only if PCs can be grievously wounded or killed in it. Obviously I won't even start it without that condition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450653#post1450653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For Werewolves then, the main challenge of the game is in <i>finding</i> the prey that's causing them issues and dealing with problems you <i>can't</i> solve by simply going Gauru.</div> </div> </div> </div>If that's the point, then most of combat is not a problem, won't and shouldn't appear in the game. That's not how I imagine Werewolf game, with combat being grisly, dangerous and often lethal affair.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450653#post1450653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> If you think about hunting animals in nature, it's typically identifying, stalking, and chasing prey that's the hard part of the hunt. Actually killing the prey usually isn't the difficult part, and in almost all cases the failure state for a hunt is that the prey <i>escapes</i>, not that the prey turns around and murders you. Actually making the kill (and getting to eat) is the <i>reward</i> of the hunt, not the challenge.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's correct... for humans. For wolves, there is very real possibility of being killed, or at least severely wounded to the point there you are going to die from infection and hunger. In fact, this was precisely the fate of Father Wolf himself. Part of reward for the hunt is not losing more than gaining - part which is all too easy for werewolves with their regeneration.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450653#post1450653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A better way to balance death rage is to <i>make it important that the player keeps control of their character</i>. There are lots of ways you can do this, but the easiest is to set things up so that the fire they're putting out right now isn't the only fire they have to put out tonight. Ten plus minutes of death rage, the resulting destruction, and then calls to emergency services, police, roadblocks etc. could be catastrophic if the characters are on a tight schedule, even if they don't get caught.</div> </div> </div> </div>All those things are meaningless in the Shadow - place where at least half of werewolf hunts are happening. You certainly can do that in Flesh... to the point. Not every battle can be reduced to &quot;keep it quiet, please&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450653#post1450653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having the pack need to run from one fire to another also lets you wear the characters down without needing to touch their health boxes. A Werewolf without essence or willpower is actually a lot less threatening than you might think, and barring oath violations, these resources come back slowly. If you have the pack face off against multiple sets of foes in a single night, even if those foes aren't a threat in and of themselves, the repeated drain on their resources might turn them into a more substantial threat.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a valid advice. Unfortunately, it does nothing with Death Rage button, which does not require Essence.<br /> <br /> In general, your advice sums up to &quot;make it inconvenient to use Gauru/fall into Death Rage&quot;. Unfortunately, the means to do that are scarce and sum up to &quot;make all battles happen in the locations with the bystanders&quot;. Which excludes all the Shadow, most of wilderness, most of abandoned places... it forces ST to constantly find ways to deprive PCs of their greatest weapon. Which is... not interesting, in my opinion, and leads to fairly contrived plots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T08:15:31-07:00","id":1450686,"title":"","content":"Gauru is meant to be the killing form, specifically there for killing blows. So you don't generally just switch to it because. That said, it's super useful with the healing so if another form isn't cutting it and retreat isn't on the table, it's likely an option werewolves will fall back on.<br /> <br /> I think you're not really disassociating the last two tiers much. Gauru without Kuruth is a thumbs up, you're powerful and smashing, you basically have to attack but you can still shift down. Gauru with Kuruth is going to be raging for at least 10 minutes. Consider that a turn is about 3 seconds, that's a lot of turns. They recommend the blackout experience for this. While you can say &quot;Well there's no humans here so no problem&quot; in werewolf that's not as much of a given in the Hisil, where suddenly turning on spirit allies and wrecking loci can lead to similar long-term ramifications as turning on random humans and wrecking buildings.<br /> <br /> So while you can be on turn 7 of Gauru, and realizing you have to shift down, there's a difference between 'It will end this fight' and 'I'm going to be killing everything I come across for 10 minutes to 12 hours.' Especially since if you haven't beaten whatever you pumped yourself up against in Stamina+Primal Urge turns, it's entirely possible being in Gauru isn't making enough of a difference. Going Kuruth at that point can be as much of a 'I guess I'll just die' button as an 'I win' one.<br /> <br /> Kuruth can cause more problems than it seems to solve from a reader's standpoint. It's designed this way, part of the drawback of being a werewolf and a reluctance to just let you use the form (with all its powers) constantly. A simple issue that comes up is once you have PCs of different Primal Urge. PC A is in Kuruth for 10 minutes, PC B is in Kuruth for 15. Once PC A falls out of it, they're another tasty morsel for their packmate.<br /> <br /> This isn't that hard to get across in play, you generally won't see people going gauru, or pushing into Kuruth, all the time. It may take them a bit to get used to their powers as is, and a demonstration via an experienced werewolf NPC can help, but if not there's always the other side of the table, where now the spirits won't deal with you, or the humans have an APB out for your human form.<br /> <br /> This paragraph kinda meanders. All that said, I do think Chronicles would benefit from offering a lot of handholding advice for combat, and how to make it interesting (and dangerous). But part of the issue is that it can be so contextual. Every gameline is going to come at combat differently, even Hunter and Werewolf, which are very similar to the point of having similar Tactics mechanics in both editions, are drastically different in that one you play a relatively fragile human and the other you play a regenerating shapeshifter. I've found that starting PCs off as mortals, then getting them through the change, can help players get a grasp of their capability a lot. It's easy to look at the books and not really feel how different it is when your PC has taken 1 Lethal and is now like more than 10% closer to death for two in-game days. And as a player, finally having your character change and realize you can just heal that in less than a scene, or with a point of energy, it's a big change. But man, the whole transformation from template to template thing isn't something you want to run every game with, it can be such a slog at times.<br /> <br /> To sum up.<br /> <br /> Spicing up combat is a subject of a lot of RPG advice columns, blogs, and youtube videos. It's been further explored in different forms of media, combat sequences in films, books, video games. There's a lot out there that can suggest ways to give satisfying story arcs with the right amount of peaks and valleys for action. Sticking with the topic of the thread I would say one thing to remember is that Kuruth is not an 'I Win' button, even if it looks like it. Character choices have consequences and while you don't always have to flip the tragedy switch, think about what would happen in other parallel settings if someone decided to just kill a whole bunch of people. But this is a lesson I think the players who've read the book and are in the pilot seat of their character will understand early on. Combat with werewolves can be over and done with fast, especially given the D&amp;D effect. 5 rounds is a long time, and 10 minutes of that is a lot of killing.<br /> <br /> There is more specific advice, I think Aurumae got into it a bit but aside from 'You don't want to be out of control with an enemy that can withstand a lot of gauru as is,' you have a variety of ways to spice things up. Complimenting Aurumae's idea, adding layers between the PCs and the BBEG is an easy and obvious one, spirit nobles have spirit minions, idigam have heralds and others borne from Essence shaping, werewolves have packs, humans have other humans (and occasionally ghouls or proximi or charmed minions), even hosts have lesser hosts and spirits or people they've suckered into working for them. Just like in fiction, having an antagonist you can sacrifice to the protagonists is a valuable asset when it comes to keeping a more plot-relevant antagonist alive. The leader has time to escape, or can lob Blasts or use Influence or other powers to affect the terrain of the battle. Tilts like Ice and Blizzard can be used to the werewolf's detriment, as well as making what was the standard white room into a white room with impact. One doesn't even have to stop with the books, obviously, and can customize Tilts so that the snow drifts piling up at your feet are necrotic somehow, sapping you of Essence or even blood (in the form of Lethal) every turn, making rollover that much more likely. Another thing that's been popping up in more combat-oriented RPGs is the staged battles, meaning that a fight with a BBEG is divided up into stages and, for example, when you've done a certain amount of damage to the priestess with spider-trappings she turns into a more drider-like creature, and upon dealing a certain amount of damage to that she goes all out, revealing her true form as a house-sized spider dripping with demonic ichor. In each of these stages the antagonist has different tactics, powers available, and presents itself as a different threat. Using the example the first form might use a gun and vastly increased Dexterity and Speed, plinking the PCs as they deal with her thugs. In the second form she can spew webs all over the place, making movement and combat difficult, maybe giving her a chance to wrap up certain opponents to get them out of the fight until they can claw their way out (or be clawed out by others). In the final form you have the biggest and baddest of forms, where bites do damage that lingers over turns, and a tarsus that can stab through concrete, potentially leading to the entire battlefield collapsing. There's ways to spice this up with things like having minions, with more obvious servants in the first stage, none in the second, then lesser azlu formed by shed shards in the third. The big thing is, this is not the kind of fight you want to be blacked out and out of control for. If your PCs haven't gotten the message (again, Kuruth not being an 'I Win' button is something I think players will understand), then having the antagonist make a very clear 'I'm going to outlast your gauru limit' sign through some delay tactic or even outright saying something like 'I can outlast you, and kill you while you're exhausted and unaware' should manage it.<br /> <br /> Obviously your goal isn't to kill the players, but if your PCs are just going Kuruth against antagonists that are meant to be powerful and smart, then you might just have them make the smart move and leave while the PCs do exhaust themselves. They don't even have to know that the antagonist isn't dead. Without examining the bodies of all the others slain during their frenzy it's possible for them to believe they killed the antagonist who is just now regathering their strength.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-02-2021, 11:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T09:17:51-07:00","id":1450692,"title":"","content":"Just understood that actually, if you go into Death Rage in Shadow, you can make quite a lot of enemies amongst local spirit courts as you rage through the Shadow. So there is at least one way to make Death Rage costly in Shadow...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T11:03:02-07:00","id":1450702,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450684#post1450684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is the problem I have. For part which is rarely the challenge, there is too much content devoted to this part. Frankly, if combat is not meant to be a challenge, then entire Auspice has nothing to do for a living (looking at you, Rahu), and neither does tremendous amount of Gifts and Merits and what-have-you. If combat is not meant to be a tactical challenge for the players, there is next to zero reason to have anything but Down-and-Dirty rules for combat, or even a sidebar with &quot;werewolves win&quot;. For me, combat has meaning if and only if PCs can be grievously wounded or killed in it. Obviously I won't even start it without that condition.<br /> <br /> All those things are meaningless in the Shadow - place where at least half of werewolf hunts are happening. You certainly can do that in Flesh... to the point. Not every battle can be reduced to &quot;keep it quiet, please&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you should separate the idea of hunts and combat. In our game, most hunts to not end in a combat, or they end with the down and dirty rules being used.<br /> <br /> As to hunting in the Shadow specifically, I would actually say that the repercussions of death raging there are most significant then the repercussions of death raging in the flesh because spirits know about werewolves (unlike mortal authorities).<br /> <br /> Your average Joe spirit can be thought of as akin to the average Joe mortal. He is rank 1 or maybe rank 2, and doesn't pose a threat to the pack, or indeed to anyone. What keeps him safe is the fact that he belongs to a social structure - the local spirit court.<br /> <br /> Most courts will tolerate a pack that is friendly or at least neutral to their goals. However, getting a bunch of your subjects murdered on a semi-regular basis is definitely going to weaken a court (very dangerous as nearby courts might try to take advantage of the situation), and eventually they are going to take actions to deal with their Uratha problem. The rank 3 or 4 spirits that rule most courts can be challenging to the pack in and of themselves, but these spirits are like feudal rulers, and can call up their vassals should the situation require it.<br /> <br /> The pack can find <i>themselves </i>hunted if they cause too much of an issue in the <i>Hisil</i>. Rank 2 spirits may not be threatening in and of themselves, but when the local Spirit monarch shows up with a host of several dozen rank 2 spirits, the Uratha are probably going to die. No amount of regeneration can escape the action economy. Keeping the local Spirit court sweet is one of those constant problems a pack has to contend with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450684#post1450684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In general, your advice sums up to &quot;make it inconvenient to use Gauru/fall into Death Rage&quot;. Unfortunately, the means to do that are scarce and sum up to &quot;make all battles happen in the locations with the bystanders&quot;. Which excludes all the Shadow, most of wilderness, most of abandoned places... it forces ST to constantly find ways to deprive PCs of their greatest weapon. Which is... not interesting, in my opinion, and leads to fairly contrived plots.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see Death Rage as a Werewolf's &quot;greatest weapon&quot;, I see it as a flaw they need to content with. In fact, in the book it's listed under &quot;The Balance: Weaknesses&quot;. Having hunts end in the wilderness or abandoned places is what they pack should want. Again, <i>most of their foes know this</i>. They aren't stupid, and aren't going to make things easy for the pack. A possessed person running into a crowded shopping mall or police station is a perfectly sensible course of action when the pack is after them. If the pack is fighting in the wilderness and can death rage freely, that probably means they planned and pulled off the hunt without any issues. If things go wrong for them, they might find themselves needing to make a plan to get into a police station or somewhere similar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-02T13:58:11-07:00","id":1450733,"title":"","content":"It feels like there's a lot of exclusion of the rest of the Uratha's forms in all this. And of course the plethora of Merits and other mechanics werewolves get (like Flaring Renown, Auspice Aspects, and Pack Tactics from The Pack).<br /> <br /> Compressing everything not Gauru into one &quot;tier&quot; is kind of ridiculous. A pack of Iron Masters kitted out in SWAT gear meant for their Dalu forms is not the same as a random group of Uratha stuck naked and without gear in Hishu. Getting piled on by Urshul form Uratha isn't some picnic.<br /> <br /> Gauru is the most potent form, sure, but in raw offensive power it's not actually that far ahead of Urshul, and Urshul doesn't have Rage to deal with. That +3 Health also means they can take a solid hit too. The three non-human non-Gauru forms give the Uratha a huge amount of tactical flexibility.<br /> <br /> Depending on the make up of the pack, some threats might really mean Gauru or bust, but the same threat might be easily something they can handle in other forms. Like any game with flexible character design, encounter design is complicated because there's no shorthand for what kind of threats are going to fall where.<br /> <br /> ------------------<br /> <br /> I'm also not really sure how much you can do about having a different of preference around the point of combat, and how WtF 2e incentivizes things. Forsaken is about hunting. The Wolf Must Hunt is the tagline of the game. The Uratha are hunters, and they're built around hunting. Violence is part of their lives, but it takes lots of forms, and not just combat. The role of combat in Forsaken is aiming for just might not be what the OP wants? Either you adjust to the game, or you figure out how to adjust the game, or you find something that suits you better.<br /> <br /> CofD, and Forsaken in particular, is not built on the idea that combat is only for when &quot;serious&quot; harm is a risk. That's why CofD stresses establishing stakes for the first to figure out things like Beat Down thresholds. If you add an extra bar of threat before you use combat, you deny yourself a tool to make the overall story interesting by going after one of the Uratha's weakness: endurance. As hunters, they are designed to end fights fast, or not win them. Making them use up resources on &quot;easy&quot; low risk fights makes the bigger fights more interesting.<br /> <br /> To an extent, it's kind of like designing a dungeon crawl. If you make every fight in the dungeon something that leaves the party drained and barely capable of moving on, you never get to the &quot;crawl&quot; part of the game. A good dungeon crawl is a series of encounters that gets harder somewhat because of increasing danger, but also because of dwindling resources. Forsaken just gives you a lot more options than combat encounters and traps to challenge the character's ability to handle their resources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T11:17:25-07:00","id":1450856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450702#post1450702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you should separate the idea of hunts and combat. In our game, most hunts to not end in a combat, or they end with the down and dirty rules being used.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that's your preference. But I asked for how to make combat more interesting while keeping it dangerous, not how to avoid it in my games or skip it.<br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450733#post1450733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It feels like there's a lot of exclusion of the rest of the Uratha's forms in all this. And of course the plethora of Merits and other mechanics werewolves get (like Flaring Renown, Auspice Aspects, and Pack Tactics from The Pack).<br /> <br /> Compressing everything not Gauru into one &quot;tier&quot; is kind of ridiculous. A pack of Iron Masters kitted out in SWAT gear meant for their Dalu forms is not the same as a random group of Uratha stuck naked and without gear in Hishu. Getting piled on by Urshul form Uratha isn't some picnic.<br /> <br /> Gauru is the most potent form, sure, but in raw offensive power it's not actually that far ahead of Urshul, and Urshul doesn't have Rage to deal with. That +3 Health also means they can take a solid hit too. The three non-human non-Gauru forms give the Uratha a huge amount of tactical flexibility.<br /> <br /> Depending on the make up of the pack, some threats might really mean Gauru or bust, but the same threat might be easily something they can handle in other forms. Like any game with flexible character design, encounter design is complicated because there's no shorthand for what kind of threats are going to fall where.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is true. However, I do feel that Gauru and especially regeneration in Gauru is strong enough put to put all those into one tier. Maybe I am wrong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450733#post1450733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you add an extra bar of threat before you use combat, you deny yourself a tool to make the overall story interesting by going after one of the Uratha's weakness: endurance. As hunters, they are designed to end fights fast, or not win them. Making them use up resources on &quot;easy&quot; low risk fights makes the bigger fights more interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is a valid piece of advice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450733#post1450733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To an extent, it's kind of like designing a dungeon crawl. If you make every fight in the dungeon something that leaves the party drained and barely capable of moving on, you never get to the &quot;crawl&quot; part of the game. A good dungeon crawl is a series of encounters that gets harder somewhat because of increasing danger, but also because of dwindling resources. Forsaken just gives you a lot more options than combat encounters and traps to challenge the character's ability to handle their resources.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that's very difference from dungeon crawls I experienced, in which most of the fights were drained and exhausted - which is why you had to plan your rest and recuperation in advance, while measuring your resources and be ready to pull back if you run out of them. Besides, Forsaken isn't a dungeon crawl game. You don't throw random encounter spirits on Forsaken while they go hunting, and unless prey is able to gain reinforcements or have/create servants on it's own, it's probably going to be the only combat encounter of pack through the chronicle.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450686#post1450686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So while you can be on turn 7 of Gauru, and realizing you have to shift down, there's a difference between 'It will end this fight' and 'I'm going to be killing everything I come across for 10 minutes to 12 hours.' Especially since if you haven't beaten whatever you pumped yourself up against in Stamina+Primal Urge turns, it's entirely possible being in Gauru isn't making enough of a difference. Going Kuruth at that point can be as much of a 'I guess I'll just die' button as an 'I win' one.<br /> <br /> Kuruth can cause more problems than it seems to solve from a reader's standpoint. It's designed this way, part of the drawback of being a werewolf and a reluctance to just let you use the form (with all its powers) constantly. A simple issue that comes up is once you have PCs of different Primal Urge. PC A is in Kuruth for 10 minutes, PC B is in Kuruth for 15. Once PC A falls out of it, they're another tasty morsel for their packmate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those are valid observations.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450686#post1450686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is more specific advice, I think Aurumae got into it a bit but aside from 'You don't want to be out of control with an enemy that can withstand a lot of gauru as is,' you have a variety of ways to spice things up. Complimenting Aurumae's idea, adding layers between the PCs and the BBEG is an easy and obvious one, spirit nobles have spirit minions, idigam have heralds and others borne from Essence shaping, werewolves have packs, humans have other humans (and occasionally ghouls or proximi or charmed minions), even hosts have lesser hosts and spirits or people they've suckered into working for them. Just like in fiction, having an antagonist you can sacrifice to the protagonists is a valuable asset when it comes to keeping a more plot-relevant antagonist alive. The leader has time to escape, or can lob Blasts or use Influence or other powers to affect the terrain of the battle. Tilts like Ice and Blizzard can be used to the werewolf's detriment, as well as making what was the standard white room into a white room with impact. One doesn't even have to stop with the books, obviously, and can customize Tilts so that the snow drifts piling up at your feet are necrotic somehow, sapping you of Essence or even blood (in the form of Lethal) every turn, making rollover that much more likely. Another thing that's been popping up in more combat-oriented RPGs is the staged battles, meaning that a fight with a BBEG is divided up into stages and, for example, when you've done a certain amount of damage to the priestess with spider-trappings she turns into a more drider-like creature, and upon dealing a certain amount of damage to that she goes all out, revealing her true form as a house-sized spider dripping with demonic ichor. In each of these stages the antagonist has different tactics, powers available, and presents itself as a different threat. Using the example the first form might use a gun and vastly increased Dexterity and Speed, plinking the PCs as they deal with her thugs. In the second form she can spew webs all over the place, making movement and combat difficult, maybe giving her a chance to wrap up certain opponents to get them out of the fight until they can claw their way out (or be clawed out by others). In the final form you have the biggest and baddest of forms, where bites do damage that lingers over turns, and a tarsus that can stab through concrete, potentially leading to the entire battlefield collapsing. There's ways to spice this up with things like having minions, with more obvious servants in the first stage, none in the second, then lesser azlu formed by shed shards in the third. The big thing is, this is not the kind of fight you want to be blacked out and out of control for. If your PCs haven't gotten the message (again, Kuruth not being an 'I Win' button is something I think players will understand), then having the antagonist make a very clear 'I'm going to outlast your gauru limit' sign through some delay tactic or even outright saying something like 'I can outlast you, and kill you while you're exhausted and unaware' should manage it.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a valid advice, and thank you for giving it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T12:30:57-07:00","id":1450865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450856#post1450856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, that's your preference. But I asked for how to make combat more interesting while keeping it dangerous, not how to avoid it in my games or skip it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The larger point though, is a lot of how to keep combat interesting is how you use it as part of the overarching structure of the game. Forsaken (and the CofD in general) doesn't really hold up to being used for a series of combat set pieces. There's a lot of tactical breadth in the system, but not a lot of depth. The stakes around the fight are going to be what keeps combat in the game interesting and engaging.<br /> <br /> &quot;You all made it to your prey with full health, almost full Essence, and haven't had to use Gauru yet. We're just going to narrate taking them down,&quot; is an important tool because you're priming the players expectations for when you do call for full combat. Simply by virtue of showing that you'll skip and avoid some fights, you can influence expectations around fights to make them more interesting just by having rolled combat because the players are now curious why this fight was rolled and the other one wasn't. Even more-so if the fight wasn't all that hard because you're communicating to them that there was a reason for the fight even if they don't see it yet.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That is true. However, I do feel that Gauru and especially regeneration in Gauru is strong enough put to put all those into one tier. Maybe I am wrong. </div> </div> </div>It feels like there's a certain amount of &quot;white room&quot; thinking going on. There are just so many factors in actual play that make this whole tier concept hard to justify as useful to understanding the game.<br /> <br /> A simple example: Does your Rahu have the Pure Condition up?<br /> <br /> If yes, Urhan is very likely to be their form if they're free to choose. The &quot;automatically go first&quot; power combined with automatic successes to hit plus whatever combat Gifts they have as a Rahu means they can completely take control over a fight before it even starts. The Pure Condition easily outpaces Gauru in terms of offense, and regeneration doesn't matter if the prey is done for before they can even do anything.<br /> <br /> If no, Gauru is far more appealing. The better overall combat stats, decent odds of still going first as long as you attack with fangs/claws, and regeneration is clearly the form you want to be fighting in if you're going to be trying to rip something to shreds ASAP anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, that's very difference from dungeon crawls I experienced,... </div> </div> </div>Perhaps a bit of terminology issue as well. Dungeon crawls are, as I know the term, not randomized, but enemies are in preset locations (one of the main jobs of the rogue-archetype is scouting them out). Hex crawls are the games that focus more on random encounters as each Hex is randomized with what sorts of threats are in it based on its type.<br /> <br /> Anyway, it's an analogy. If you're talking about planning your rests and knowing when to retreat, the point was made. Forsaken is a game where resource management is part of keeping things interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T17:49:04-07:00","id":1450898,"title":"","content":"I find myself wondering if spirit prey might not have things they can throw at insufficiently cautious Uratha that their regeneration doesn't protect them from. Particular Numina or the effects of certain Influences that might make life a bit harder when they come away from the hunt for a while. I'd need to check back over the rules for how those things are resisted and what the durations are. <br /> <br /> At least as far as a bit of tension for what the spirit might do if one doesn't undertake actions that will pin it down and then effectively pull the trigger on it.<br /> <br /> I think the statement about fights taking place in the Shadow needs to consider that spirits might have Manifestations to cross over and look for somewhere that werewolves will need to go to more effort to use Gauru discretely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-03T19:36:41-07:00","id":1450902,"title":"","content":"While there's obviously the go-to of silver, it's the kind of thing that can be done with Tilts inflicted via Numina or Influences. Something that inflicts 1 Lethal damage per turn on everyone in the room, or a Dread Power like Fire Elemental that causes 3 Lethal to everything near it every turn. These things act to help push rollover, and it's the sort of thing that is going to basically encourage the werewolves to go into Gauru. This can be used to tire them out, since you can only take Gauru once per scene (barring rage), and act as a good 'softener' before another antagonist, especially if they're of the more fragile sort themselves.<br /> <br /> In our last game I used snake hosts, and they were going to be fought in a fairly small, closed in area. Not ideal for the hosts but once they discorporate it left the floor covered in venomous snakes that I felt was best represented by a Tilt that required the PCs to choose between moving carefully and thus slowly and with a penalty to Speed nd Dexterity or moving regularly (like when fighting) and getting bitten and poisoned.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/\" target=\"_blank\">This guy meanders in his posts, but he knows RPGs. Specifically Dungeons and Dragons, unfortunately, but a lot of his advice for spicing up combat can be refigured for Werewolf (and Chronicles in general) to really spice up combat. It helps that he looks at the game as stories and combat as part of the narrative so, mechanics aside, he's trying to make battles as interesting as possible as well.</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450898#post1450898\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the statement about fights taking place in the Shadow needs to consider that spirits might have Manifestations to cross over and look for somewhere that werewolves will need to go to more effort to use Gauru discretely.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/\" target=\"_blank\">T</a>his can be an easy threat to make, as well. Just open a Gauntlet Breach and let the werewolves know if they rage they'll find themselves raging in midtown, or wherever.<br /> <br /> There's a lot of inspiration for this stuff in books and movies but really a solid source tends to be video games. I played the recent-ish God of War for the first time last month and by the end of it I was fighting someone who effectively had Influence 5 Frost and Fire, while on top of an undead giant bent on separating us or smashing me. Kratos can rage, but it's very brief, the enemy survives. If Kratos were Uratha? Antagonist'd disappear, I'd attack the giant futilely, and probably end up frozen under the ice for a thousand years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-03-2021, 11:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T03:49:56-07:00","id":1450942,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450902#post1450902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://&amp;quot;https://theangrygm.com/series/run-better-battles/&amp;quot;\" target=\"_blank\">T</a>his can be an easy threat to make, as well. Just open a Gauntlet Breach and let the werewolves know if they rage they'll find themselves raging in midtown, or wherever.</div> </div> </div> </div>Although that particular one requires at least a Rank 3 spirit, so not necessarily a standard danger.<br /> <br /> Still, the subject makes me think of the first fight in Captain America: Civil War. Crossbones gets a few decent hits in and presents a nifty and menacing weapon (plus using an opening move that disables his opponent's main weapon), but taken on the whole one can see a portrayal in which Cap completely outmatches him. This being the first fight of a two hour movie, you're not necessarily looking for tension in the sense that the hero might lose. Instead, you get it from the particular manner in which he wins, and how the consequences of that play through the rest of the story.<br /> <br /> I think that can be a relevant thing to use in something like a Chronicles of Darkness game, especially Werewolf, where one implements things like the cultural context. A cornered spirit might not be able to kill a pack in close quarters that has gone war form, but things it might do with magic in desperation that the pack did not appropriately prepare for might cause complications in their lives or their standing with other werewolves. Maybe not in the long term, but it could be incitement for something that becomes long term. I think that's apparent in game advice about werewolves being compelled to partially eat other werewolves, especially to get around the troublesome regeneration.<br /> <br /> Although there's also a question of a spirit making lateral moves when under threat. Take the fight to a complex environment that might aid in evasion (especially with the right Influence) until the Rage runs out. If it's a spirit that could employ the Hallucinate Numen, maybe give Raging werewolves something imaginary to go crazy at. If you're not equipped to meet force with force, consider means of redirecting or dispersing it.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure what the original poster had in mind for prey of real life wolves that poses a threat of injury to them, but one should also consider footage of the rabbit that survives a wolf attack through superior agility, darting about and changing direction in ways that confuse wolves, make their own momentum carry them away from the quarry, and slip through gaps in the net.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T06:05:12-07:00","id":1450951,"title":"","content":"Rank 3 is where spirits become that wise enough to do something like that, before then if you're having trouble with them it's more likely due to numbers than to any power or intelligence factor. Rank 1s and 2s are comparable to animals and children, and are likely going to be cowed by confronting a werewolf in Gauru anyhow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T06:36:50-07:00","id":1450952,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450951#post1450951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rank 1s and 2s are comparable to animals and children, and are likely going to be cowed by confronting a werewolf in Gauru anyhow.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah but I think one has to consider the way animals react when in danger, even to the point of being terrified for their lives, and apply that mindset to capabilities of an elemental or conceptual nature.<br /> <br /> I don't think using the Hallucinate Numen to create something for an attacker to focus on instead implies sophisticated intelligence any more than detaching a tail or assembling the colourful markings on wings to look like a big scary face does.<br /> <br /> I think Rank 3 is the point at which you're using Hallucinate to make werewolves perform major social faux pas, not basic survival.<br /> <br /> Plus the idea that they might flail desperately with an Influence.<br /> <br /> I think perhaps one could play some of the local low Rank spirits as acting with a bit of rudimentary training from the bigger spirits as a guard dog might receive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T09:14:19-07:00","id":1450965,"title":"","content":"How to keep Werewolf combat interesting? Here are some thoughts<br /> <br /> 1/ The Pure: The first edition really went into detail about how dangerous the Pure were. They were more numerous than the Forsaken (outnumbering Forsaken about 2:1), highly organized, and brutal as hell. Blood of the Wolf was going on about demographics and said that a fight between a pack of Forsaken, and a Pack of Pure that outnumbered them by even a few members was going to be a hell of a fight. So...if Werewolves are the baddest of the bad, throw more Werewolves at them. Pure vs. Forsaken is an apples to apples comparison. They know the weaknesses of the Forsaken and can fight on even terms. If there are more Pure than Forsaken, that will add lots of tension to the fight<br /> <br /> 2/ Silver: Just use Silver. Craft a silver knife, load a shotgun with Silver Buckshot, use a piece of silverware. Unlike Kryptonite, Silver is everywhere and humans mine thousands of tons of it per year. A little old lady with a silver Letter opener can become a danger to a careless forsaken. If you want inspiration, read the fiction in the Idigam chronicles. There is one part where they said that silver bullets don't work because silver warps in the air, but a silver shotgun is fair game because the pellets scatter so accuracy is not an issue. Then there is a part where a Pure Kinfolk kills a Blood Talon Rahu with 2 shotgun blasts. Silver weakness is an open secret too for Werewolves.<br /> <br /> Even outside of combat, it can be socially inconvenient to be in an antique shop and your character starts screaming because he accidently picked up a silver coin.<br /> <br /> 3: Boost your Spirits: Spirits and boost attributes by Rank + 2, pump up your attributes and even a rank 2 spirit can be a tough fight and a pack of them can dogpile and bring down even a werewolf in Kuruth<br /> <br /> 4: Emotional Aura Numien: This is a debuff which reduces dicepools of everyone within 5 yards by 2. Suddenly the Garou form is neutralized somewhat and is not so tough now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T14:07:13-07:00","id":1450983,"title":"","content":"This is sort of another thing I see a lot of questions about 'balancing combat' for. Basically it's very easy for Storytellers to create an antagonist that gets melted down by the protagonists because the ST wasn't aware of the damage the PCs were capable of. You do have the other end of the table with a 'rocks fall everyone dies' or snipers waiting a thousand yards outside of PCs houses ready to blow their heads off the moment they step outside but that is far more unlikely.<br /> <br /> As a Storyteller, if you're wanting to make engaging combats by the rules, you're going to want to know what your PCs are capable of. Just look at their sheets, and check out their combat dicepools and their health. Our first game in Werewolf second edition, I wanted to avoid a previous character's overpoweredness (he was built with Strength and Athletics, being an athlete, but in first edition this means you can basically one shot an elephant with a bow), by making a character who would not be a combat machine. So I had no combat Skills. The ST encouraged me to put at least one dot somewhere and I put it in Brawl. This doesn't seem like much. He had 2 Strength, 1 Brawl. In a bar fight he would maybe be doing 1 Bashing damage, if he was lucky enough to hit considering higher Defenses in second edition, and going with the statistic that with 3 dice you're likely to get 1 success.<br /> <br /> However, as a shapeshifter, everything changes very easily. In most forms he's got a bonus to Strength, and he starts inflicting Lethal damage with his attacks, to the point that in Gauru form he's got +3 to Strength, and for every success he's doing +2L damage and negating Athletics, the big boost to Defense in second edition. This means that the skimpy looking weakling who was throwing down maybe 1 Bashing if he was lucky is more likely to get 2 Successes, and do an extra two Lethal on top of that, giving him something like 4 Lethal damage for one attack. Add something like All-Out for +2, and Willpower for +3, and you're likely filling your average 7 Health human in one shot, if you don't decide to just go Down &amp; Dirty. And that's without any special Fighting Styles or other combat-oriented Merits, with one dot of Skill.<br /> <br /> So, my advice for that is to look at your PCs sheets and write down their likely combat dicepools and their Health with a range. My character, Malcolm White was pretty simple. Brawl 3 to 6, +0B to +2L, 7 to 11 Health. Now I know at a glance and with some quick maths what they're likely to be doing in case I'm running Malcolm and have him attacked by a single entity and I want it to be a challenge. You can always make antagonists tougher by playing on action economy by offering them in a group, or make a single antagonist tougher by adding Defense (which can make them unhittable at points, like above 6 for Malcolm if he's not using WP or All-Out attacks) or Armor (which reduces the damage, making the fight last longer but still keeping the antagonist within the range of hittability, like general armor 3 turns his 4 Lethal to 1), or a mix of all three. And once the other packmates get into the fight, I'd be looking at a list of damage they're capable of.<br /> <br /> There's a lot of ways it can be upended, of course, Gifts, tactics, banes, and planning can melt some of the toughest enemies. But I've found this can help a long way for STs who aren't sure about the 'power levels' of what they're making, or the PCs they're running.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T19:18:56-07:00","id":1450999,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450965#post1450965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> There is one part where they said that silver bullets don't work because silver warps in the air, but a silver shotgun is fair game because the pellets scatter so accuracy is not an issue.</div> </div> </div> </div>Silver is harder than lead, it wouldn't &quot;warp in the air&quot; any more than a regular bullet would. Even if that were true, you could just use a jacketed hollow-point to deliver the silver without loss of accuracy. <br /> <br /> But the basic point is entirely sound; silver is readily available and very deadly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T19:51:06-07:00","id":1451003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1450999#post1450999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Silver is harder than lead, it wouldn't &quot;warp in the air&quot; any more than a regular bullet would. Even if that were true, you could just use a jacketed hollow-point to deliver the silver without loss of accuracy.<br /> <br /> But the basic point is entirely sound; silver is readily available and very deadly.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> thanks for the input, I was not sure if it could be used in firearms. Could it be made at home in a mould ( say by some ghouls...)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T20:13:22-07:00","id":1451006,"title":"","content":"Apparently silver has some faults as bullet material because it has less mass than lead of the same dimensions and the hardness can affect how it interacts with barrel rifling, which can result less penetration and accuracy. Although it's not exactly a rigorously tested matter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T20:16:16-07:00","id":1451007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451003#post1451003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> thanks for the input, I was not sure if it could be used in firearms. Could it be made at home in a mould ( say by some ghouls...)?</div> </div> </div> </div>Filling a shotgun shell is no real problem at all. Filling a hollow or explosive point bullet with silver would take a little more effort and basic gunsmithing equipment. Making a reliable and accurate full-fledged silver bullet would take a custom-mold and a fair amount of gunsmithing experience. Oddly a well-made silver round might have so <i>little </i>deformation it over penetrates your target and isn't as deadly as you'd hope, since you want the silver to stay inside the werewolf. A proper silver round would be a little less accurate at longer ranges and more likely to pass through a target without fragmenting than an equivalent normal bullet.<br /> <br /> If you're just desperate and in a hurry, making a crappy silver bullet would be relatively easy, but you'd run risk of the round not cycling properly or having crap accuracy, that sort of stuff.<br /> <br /> In game terms, for a well-made silver bullet, use the -1 damage they had in 1st Edition. For a hollow point or similar round that is just delivering the silver rather than being the entire bullet, use the standard 2nd edition damage. For a silver bullet handmade without the right equipment and knowledge, -1 damage and lower range by one step. Maybe even treat as an Improvised Weapon; -1 damage, -1 initiative.<br /> <br /> It can also matter what kind of silver is used; a lot of silver coins are composed of alloys intended to make them harder than guns are usually designed for. Raw silver bullion is softer and will take to rifling fine. Ironically the movie &quot;Silver Bullet&quot; gave us all the misleading impression that melting down silver jewelry was the way to go. What you actually want to do is just order regular old normal silver and cast it yourself. Melted down jewelry silver is much more likely to jam your gun.<br /> <br /> I can totally see a ghoul carving out a useful skillset as &quot;that guy who makes silver bullets for the Invictus.&quot; That guy probably makes hollow-point rounds loaded with silver most of the time, fills shotgun shells with silver shot the rest of the time, and maybe does the occasional special-order for something that has to get the silver past body armor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 06-04-2021, 11:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T21:54:20-07:00","id":1451018,"title":"","content":"Silver is also fairly expensive. Depending on where your game is set, lots of mooks with guns firing normal bullets can be more effective (esp. if you're willing to do some illegal or grey-area modding to them) and less costly. In other places, getting a bunch of high end rifles, kits, and so on is a lot compared to a hunting rifle and a few silver bullets.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-04T23:58:58-07:00","id":1451022,"title":"","content":"Plus werewolves can use full Defense against Firearms and Gifts to shut them down.<br /> <br /> That said, I honestly recommend against using silver to just make combat more interesting. Aggravated damage lingers for a few days, and so it's not just something that's going to make one encounter interesting. Dangerous, sure, but in this case silver is better used by lesser thugs. The above werewolf, Malcolm, had to infiltrate a vampire elder's lair and get past a number of ghouls armed with silver guns. At that point he had really figured out how to bank on his bite attack, doing Aggravated in Urshul form to disarm targets via their Arm Wrack, usually resulting in the target missing an arm entirely (along with whatever was held in it). Still, while it was relatively easy to sneak around and kill the ones necessary, the pressure was more how a gun could set off an alarm and wake the daysleeping vampire, as well as have a bunch of ghouls that were leisurely on guard suddenly searching for an enemy and defending the entry to their master's lair. Silver acted as more of a deterrent to the 'charge in and chomp them all' style, and added to the tension of my character's stealth mission, keep in mind too that silver sets off Kuruth so it would have ruined the stealthy search and the entire pack's plan and reason why we split up in the first place. This is like what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2384_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2384\" data-vbnamecard=\"2384\">Aurumae</a> was saying, sometimes it's sort of the journey rather than the fight that makes the interesting bit. Because staking an unconscious vampire isn't a spectacularly interesting or dangerous scene. Getting past trained, ghouled guards with silver weapons, then figuring out where in the beat up old farmhouse with what seemed to be regular farmers and their dog the elder was laired, having a shotgun pointed at my character by one while the other was picking up a phone and having to decide which to go after, it's the situation that leads to choices like that that make things fun.<br /> <br /> Games like the Arkham franchise, or Metal Gear Solid, often can be used as inspiration for these things. They keep track of how you most often deal with little outposts of baddies, and eventually beef up those baddies so that your usual 'style' meets some hitches. Apparently for MGS it's called the <a href=\"https://metalgear.fandom.com/wiki/Revenge_System_(enemy_preparedness)\" target=\"_blank\">Revenge System</a>. Not all werewolf games are going to have you facing off against a ton of different groups of minions working for the same antagonist but you, as Storyteller, can see what the players are doing often, and easily, and the next time you're planning something throw some bumps in the road for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-05-2021, 03:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-07T08:25:20-07:00","id":1451215,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451018#post1451018\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Silver is also fairly expensive. Depending on where your game is set, lots of mooks with guns firing normal bullets can be more effective (esp. if you're willing to do some illegal or grey-area modding to them) and less costly. In other places, getting a bunch of high end rifles, kits, and so on is a lot compared to a hunting rifle and a few silver bullets.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You think? More expensive than normal bullets, but a reasonable investment if you happen to go to war with Uratha and want an efficient way to fight them. Considering an ounce of silver costs about $28.00 as of today, and lets assume you need an ounce to load a shotgun, I am sure the Uratha would be dismayed to find out that they can be laid low for a low price of about $60. I am thinking that a silver knife can probably be moulded for about $100.00. Again, a bit pricy, but a reasonable investment for getting the job done. Even one silver bullet can make the massed &quot;roll over&quot; damage much easier since it eliminates a number of health levels to hurdle. Besides, the Chronicles of Darkness are filled with Invictus and Mummy cults which probably have access to obscene amounts of wealth to make it a possibility.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-07T09:27:20-07:00","id":1451222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451215#post1451215\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More expensive than normal bullets,</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Considering, depending on the gun, a single bullet shot can cost between $0.20 to $1, $60 is a lot.<br /> <br /> For ~$60 you could supply the ammo for five mooks to spray then entire Uratha pack with automatic fire potentially doing ~20L damage in a single round to each member of the pack. As opposed to possibly killing, maybe just really hurting and pissing off, one Uratha for the same price.<br /> <br /> And having a decently trained firearms based hit squad that isn't reliant on specialized ammo is more flexible for anyone that might bump into more than Uratha.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I am thinking that a silver knife can probably be moulded for about $100.00. </div> </div> </div>It's going to be a lot more than $100 worth of silver. The blade itself, not accounting for everything else you need to make a decent fighting knife, is going to be more like $325 for a basic fighting knife.<br /> <br /> Though I never get the obsession with silver knives (outside of pop-culture cool). Silver's utility for guns might have a case for it, but there's no question it's useless for blades once it gets too pure. Pure silver can't hold an edge or be hardened. Sterling silver (which is an alloy but good enough by WtF 2e standards) can at least be turned into a functional blade, but the odds of one lasting more than one hit are obnoxiously low. Unless you're a super sneaky assassin type that's expecting to get the drop on a werewolf and slit their throat or something, silver blades make no sense.<br /> <br /> Just make a silver hammer or mace. No edge to maintain, a bit of deformation on the striking surface isn't going to render it non-functional as a weapon, and so on. It still does Agg to Uratha. If you had to use aluminum as a weapon, you wouldn't go make a knife out of it, you'd just pick up a freaking metal baseball bat. Same principles apply.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Besides, the Chronicles of Darkness are filled with Invictus and Mummy cults which probably have access to obscene amounts of wealth to make it a possibility. </div> </div> </div>But they'd also have the resources to have some PMCs on retainer too (if not outright own the company).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-07T13:52:11-07:00","id":1451254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451222#post1451222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just make a silver hammer or mace. .</div> </div> </div> </div>Good Point! Simplicity. I thought that going to a ring shop and buying large silver rings might also count as brass knuckles. A Silver Candlestick can count as an improvised weapon and get a good hit or 2 in before it falls apart. I am sure even just breaking down a silver candlestick and sharpening it can probably make a good impromptu weapon. I guess in the Chronicles of darkness, one can make a duel between a Vampire and an Uratha going into hand to hand combat.<br /> <br /> But your points are valid. I often thought that most combat starts with an ambush anyways. Get some mooks with automatic rifles to spray an Uratha while he is in human form and you can probably end the fight then and there. The core book has locations such as Basra (Shiite militias and Iraqi army and security forces), Belfast (ex-IRA), and Detroit (gangs), were you have tons of humans with combat experience and are armed.<br /> <br /> Where silver weapons, in my humble opinion, would be more useful are for for hosts and claimed, entities who are more likely to engage an Uratha in brutal combat and need an edge. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-07T14:00:53-07:00","id":1451256,"title":"","content":"Attempts to pinpoint a werewolf in a crowd in human form are penalized by their Primal Urge, plus silver is a death rage trigger that's basically going to turn them into Kuruth, part of the problem OP is trying to avoid in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-08T07:31:32-07:00","id":1451312,"title":"","content":"Recapping everything that was said in this thread:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Falling in Kuruth is not a boost, it's a problem. It should always have bad consequences for pack as a whole, unless pack was very successful in catching prey up with their pants down.</li> <li>Gauru is not much better than Urshul offensive-wise, it's more powerful defensive-wise due to having regeneration.</li> <li>Wear them down before the fight with poking strikes; bleed out Essence before main strike.</li> <li>Use action economy. Swarm Uratha in lesser enemies and even Gauru would not be able to keep up with damage piling up.</li> <li>Use continuous damage. Being poisoned, drowned, on fire and so on reduces regen factor.</li> <li>Apply debuff tilts/reduce combat dice. Being blinded, knocked down, hammered with debuff Numina and so on reduces Uratha ability to deal damage.</li> <li>Bring a healing factor of your own. If werewolves cannot deal a lot of damage and you can heal it, you'll be able to either force them into Kuruth or wear them down.</li> <li>Do not engage werewolves on their terms; avoid melee, apply debuffs and continuous damage, and most importantly, be ready to call it a day and run away faster than werewolves can follow you, especially if you managed to put them into Kuruth.</li> <li>Silver is a nuclear option. Use it sparingly.</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-08T11:17:07-07:00","id":1451360,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451312#post1451312\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Recapping everything that was said in this thread:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Falling in Kuruth is not a boost, it's a problem. It should always have bad consequences for pack as a whole, unless pack was very successful in catching prey up with their pants down.</li> <li>Gauru is not much better than Urshul offensive-wise, it's more powerful defensive-wise due to having regeneration.</li> <li>Wear them down before the fight with poking strikes; bleed out Essence before main strike.</li> <li>Use action economy. Swarm Uratha in lesser enemies and even Gauru would not be able to keep up with damage piling up.</li> <li>Use continuous damage. Being poisoned, drowned, on fire and so on reduces regen factor.</li> <li>Apply debuff tilts/reduce combat dice. Being blinded, knocked down, hammered with debuff Numina and so on reduces Uratha ability to deal damage.</li> <li>Bring a healing factor of your own. If werewolves cannot deal a lot of damage and you can heal it, you'll be able to either force them into Kuruth or wear them down.</li> <li>Do not engage werewolves on their terms; avoid melee, apply debuffs and continuous damage, and most importantly, be ready to call it a day and run away faster than werewolves can follow you, especially if you managed to put them into Kuruth.</li> <li>Silver is a nuclear option. Use it sparingly.</li> </ol></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Looks good! I was thinking that an &quot;interesting and dangerous&quot; combat situation can involve even an old lady or mook with a Silver Letter opener who is freaking out due to Lunacy. There is no doubt that the pack will defeat the person in question, but the possibility that the old lady can do even one level of damage through an all out attack and cause a Kuruth trigger might force the pack to act more tactically to somehow disarm the subject without getting too close.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T22:54:50-07:00","id":1451525,"title":"","content":"The main challenges with silver bullets are that they require extra time, cost, knowledge and equipment to make in any reliable fashion. Special work is probably also required on the weapon itself, to make sure the bullets don't break when firing.<br /> <br /> An alternative is to give silver to wolf blooded. If they spend a lot of time crossing the Gauntlet back and forth, eventually it becomes lunargent, which can be worked into amalgams without losing its werewolf damaging powers.<br /> <br /> If the hunters have a good diplomat, they can have a mage enchant the silver bullets to reduce the bleed out damage interval of the target. A Ruling of Time could reduce it by half for each level of potency, or to a quarter for a Reach. Suddenly the bullet does massive damage to even the most resilient werewolf.<br /> <br /> Lastly, never underestimate the power of a scientifically inclined adversary. Spray the werewolves with hydrogen peroxide (using a rigged sprinkler system, hose, even water balloons and water guns work). Then hit them with silver bullets, which causes it to ignite. So now they have to deal with the damage from silver while burning and perhaps being locked down by covering fire.<br /> <br /> A well prepared pack of hunters, even mortal ones, can be a nightmare for werewolves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":24280,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","user":"KaiserAfini","date":". 06-10-2021, 01:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T23:35:29-07:00","id":1451528,"title":"","content":"A werewolf that's been triggered by silver isn't going to be locked down by covering fire, since it's going to be attacking every round and using it's full defense against firearms. Also wow don't put hydrogen peroxide concentrate in sprinkler systems as silver is not the only metal it reacts to and the moment it hits a pipe you're dealing with your own ignition.<br /> <br /> I appreciate that whenever combat is mentioned in the werewolf forum people like to pop up with their A-Team operators who have armies of people with silver bullets ready to ambush werewolves, while ignoring basically everything about them aside from silver being their bane, but it's not really relevant to the thread. You can have a meteorite of pure silver just smash into their heads and kill them, too. Even astronomy can be a nightmare for werewolves. But it doesn't make combat <b><i>dangerous </i></b>or <b><i>interesting</i></b>. It ignores combat (and, again, basically everything leading up to it) and just tries to kill the PCs. You don't need silver or hunters for that, you can have them spontaneously combust or die of congenital heart failure.<br /> <br /> This is why, as Dark Archon figured out pretty quickly, it's a nuclear option. It sends them into Kuruth. And it doesn't heal fast. A creature with the Fire Elemental Dread Power is doing 3 Lethal to everything within a yard of it. A PC has to choose to fight it. Gauru gives you a lot of room to deal with it, healing all Bashing and Lethal every turn, but you're closer to that wraparound in case there are other combatants nearby. A PC still has agency there. The Rahu grapples it so it doesn't get close to their more fragile Ithaeur. He goes Gauru because he has more Health to begin with. And once it's killed, it's pressure eased, allowing focus to be shifted and a sense of achievement at being closer to battle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T07:18:04-07:00","id":1451537,"title":"","content":"Being on fire is continuous damage, which is helpful for keeping regen factor at check. What sucks about setting werewolves on fire is that they prefer to fight in melee (most of them, anyway), and once werewolf has grappled you, you are on fire too. That may not be your largest problem, given that you are going to be literally ripped apart in seconds, but fire doesn't help your case. It's like setting zombies on fire - probably a good idea, if you can keep your distance from them.<br /> <br /> Now, I have an interesting idea, which is mostly &quot;firing them down while running away from them&quot;. Imagine having a truck with machinegun on it (to invoke spirit of Makhno, write &quot;you can't run away&quot; on front and &quot;you can't catch me&quot; on back), and driving away from the pack while firing on them. Add a couple more people firing assault rifles or shotguns for more dakka. Spirits can probably do that by moving away at full speed from weres while Blasting them. This basically forces to use Urhan or Urshul, but I am not sure how this would be played out by using CofD rules. Is that possible to have chase and fight going on at the same time? Did you make a scene like that in your games?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T07:42:20-07:00","id":1451539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451537#post1451537\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Being on fire is continuous damage, which is helpful for keeping regen factor at check. What sucks about setting werewolves on fire is that they prefer to fight in melee (most of them, anyway), and once werewolf has grappled you, you are on fire too. That may not be your largest problem, given that you are going to be literally ripped apart in seconds, but fire doesn't help your case. It's like setting zombies on fire - probably a good idea, if you can keep your distance from them.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Fire Elemental Dread Power \u2014 the source of &quot;set them on fire&quot; in this discussion \u2014 reduces damage taken from fire by a substantial amount for obvious reasons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T08:00:50-07:00","id":1451542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451539#post1451539\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Fire Elemental Dread Power \u2014 the source of &quot;set them on fire&quot; in this discussion \u2014 reduces damage taken from fire by a substantial amount for obvious reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was referring more to <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini\">KaiserAfini</a></b> suggestion to set werewolves on fire with chemicals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-10T11:37:01-07:00","id":1451557,"title":"","content":"Our first sacred hunt in second edition had the target in a vehicle and shutting them down is very easy for werewolves. To be fair, it is for many supernaturals, to the point that in DaveB's actual plays it became sort of a trope that if you were going after them in a vehicle you were all going to die, and one of the playtests for Werewolf second edition faced a Rahu against a tank. If a werewolf needed to stop you or had the appropriate Gifts or other powers, like Command Artifice or having an Irraka on the team who could just teleport onto the vehicle, then the plan could be cut short. If they didn't, they could simply not chase after the guns, and use their tracking abilities to find out where it and, later, all its occupants went, or allow the police to do so.<br /> <br /> In our first hunt we just had Command Artifice pull the car into an alley where we finished things.<br /> <br /> This is where the white rooming gets more complex, as werewolves have a wide range of powers and capabilities beyond regeneration. If you are planning for werewolf PCs, see their Gift lists and play up or around them. There's an entire Lodge dedicated to hunting on the roads in Mad Max-esque fashion.<br /> <br /> If you wanted, you could use the chase scene rules from the Chronicles core and have the gunfire and clawing available to whoever has the Edge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-10-2021, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T00:13:46-07:00","id":1451616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451557#post1451557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Our first sacred hunt in second edition had the target in a vehicle and shutting them down is very easy for werewolves. To be fair, it is for many supernaturals, to the point that in DaveB's actual plays it became sort of a trope that if you were going after them in a vehicle you were all going to die, and one of the playtests for Werewolf second edition faced a Rahu against a tank. If a werewolf needed to stop you or had the appropriate Gifts or other powers, like Command Artifice or having an Irraka on the team who could just teleport onto the vehicle, then the plan could be cut short. If they didn't, they could simply not chase after the guns, and use their tracking abilities to find out where it and, later, all its occupants went, or allow the police to do so.</div> </div> </div> </div>It was a news to me that Command Artifice wasn't resisted by vehicle in any way, especially given that Cunning and Glory give a chance to resist the Gift, while Command Artifice, being potentially much more dangerous, just works. I'll be fixing that with homerules in my games, from now on, thanks. By the way, how did the tank fared against Rahu? I'd say tank cannon is able to kill absolute most of werewolves with one shot due +20 damage or so from main cannon, but they would need to be able to hit the Rahu.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T07:44:20-07:00","id":1451659,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451616#post1451616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was a news to me that Command Artifice wasn't resisted by vehicle in any way, especially given that Cunning and Glory give a chance to resist the Gift, while Command Artifice, being potentially much more dangerous, just works. I'll be fixing that with homerules in my games, from now on, thanks.</div> </div> </div> </div>That seems unnecessary. Are you planning on going through all the powers and attempting to limit them? Powers that involve dealing with the spirits of targets tend to sidestep resistances, since they're not targeting anything with a resistance. A car or gun doesn't have a mind of its own, after all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By the way, how did the tank fared against Rahu? I'd say tank cannon is able to kill absolute most of werewolves with one shot due +20 damage or so from main cannon, but they would need to be able to hit the Rahu. </div> </div> </div>Apparently the Rahu fared well. Between extra Health and Crimson Spasm they're capable of great Defense and Health. And obviously tearing apart a tank isn't that difficult.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T07:55:13-07:00","id":1451664,"title":"","content":"\u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451659#post1451659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are you planning on going through all the powers and attempting to limit them?</div> </div> </div> </div>Only those which are going to be used in game, when I discover issues with them. And gain - Garble and Unmake are being resisted if Gift if used when item is used. I'd imagine it have to work the same way for the Command Artifice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451659#post1451659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And obviously tearing apart a tank isn't that difficult.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that's not quite obvious to me. With Durability \u00b125, Size 20 and Structure \u00b145 I would actually imagine it would be quite hard to tear it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T08:10:32-07:00","id":1451667,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451664#post1451664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bOnly those which are going to be used in game, when I discover issues with them. And gain - Garble and Unmake are being resisted if Gift if used when item is used. I'd imagine it have to work the same way for the Command Artifice.</div> </div> </div> </div>Unmake is only contested if the item is being used. Garble targets a person specifically.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, that's not quite obvious to me. With Durability \u00b125, Size 20 and Structure \u00b145 I would actually imagine it would be quite hard to tear it. </div> </div> </div>Living Weapon lets you ignore any non-magical armor. Shaping, Entropy's Howl, all of these make metal barriers useless. And unless a tank is claimed all you would need to do is get the people inside, and getting it to open up is even easier than that. Or one can just incinerate the occupants from the inside. There's not that many gifts in the core but they are pretty comprehensive. Shunned gives even more options.<br /> <br /> A big thrust of the game is about celebrating the awesome (it's in the ten commandments for STs), not hitting them with made up rules because they're doing something deemed too powerful. Their antagonists tend to be high ranking enemies. Random drive-bys shouldn't really lead to the end of a character unless they've specifically built a very weak and slow character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T08:34:31-07:00","id":1451669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451667#post1451667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Living Weapon lets you ignore any non-magical armor.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't make those 45 points of Structure go away, even if you forget that it ignores Armor and not Structure (I would make a difference here, yeah). You are going to need some time clawing through that, and tank can just crush you in the meantime. Also, that's a five dots in a Merit - not exactly what I would call &quot;common&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451667#post1451667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Entropy's Howl</div> </div> </div> </div>2 Structure points of damage per success on roll. You'll need around 20 successes to &quot;make metal barrier useless&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451667#post1451667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Shaping</div> </div> </div> </div>Sculpt is sensitive to Size, but is more useful in that situation, I agree.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451667#post1451667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A big thrust of the game is about celebrating the awesome (it's in the ten commandments for STs), not hitting them with made up rules because they're doing something deemed too powerful. Their antagonists tend to be high ranking enemies. Random drive-bys shouldn't really lead to the end of a character unless they've specifically built a very weak and slow character.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there is big difference between &quot;celebrating the awesome&quot; and having protagonists which can't be meaningfully challenged without throwing a minor god at them. There is nothing awesome in fight in which you can't lose. And random drive-by's aren't made by a <i>tank.</i> So I don't see anything bad about nerfing things to be less of &quot;I win&quot; button and more interesting plot/combat devices.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T09:21:13-07:00","id":1451679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451669#post1451669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there is big difference between &quot;celebrating the awesome&quot; and having protagonists which can't be meaningfully challenged without throwing a minor god at them. There is nothing awesome in fight in which you can't lose. And random drive-by's aren't made by a <i>tank.</i> So I don't see anything bad about nerfing things to be less of &quot;I win&quot; button and more interesting plot/combat devices.</div> </div> </div> </div>The tank was just a test situation, using a heavily optimized kind of werewolf that's designed to take strong hits. The drive-bys were referring to your previous post about having a car shooting at them and driving away.<br /> <br /> Werewolves are god-slayers. It's part of their history, and what they were made to do. While a bunch of Rank 2 spirits or cunning rats might take one down, especially when it comes to numbers, in general and as a pack they are designed to face much larger or expansive threats. The idigam written up in the book are there to represent the serious kind of dangers they face. There's an entire section in Shunned by the Moon detailing the build-up of a chronicle that doesn't have players facing people shooting from a car (even if it's a bunch of people, and shooting silver), which would represent more of a mid-hunt obstacle than a pack-ending threat.<br /> <br /> This is all to say, if you want to run the game you might be best served to check out the books. We've had people posting on the forum flabberghasted that their intricate mystery plot was solved with ease because they weren't aware of the capabilities of their Mage players, one of whom had enough Death arcana to just make the ghost, a point of the mystery, do whatever they wanted. All the advice offered in the thread won't matter if you have an entire pack face down something as mundane as a gang of humans with guns. They'd get hit with the hard side of the Down &amp; Dirty treatment if it even got to gauru, even if the same group could easily kill me in real life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-11-2021, 12:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T13:07:36-07:00","id":1451724,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451679#post1451679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Werewolves are god-slayers. It's part of their history, and what they were made to do. While a bunch of Rank 2 spirits or cunning rats might take one down, especially when it comes to numbers, in general and as a pack they are designed to face much larger or expansive threats. The idigam written up in the book are there to represent the serious kind of dangers they face. There's an entire section in Shunned by the Moon detailing the build-up of a chronicle that doesn't have players facing people shooting from a car (even if it's a bunch of people, and shooting silver), which would represent more of a mid-hunt obstacle than a pack-ending threat.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is subtle, but important difference between &quot;interesting/dangerous combat&quot; and &quot;pack-ending threat&quot;. Second one forces players to show their best game, otherwise it's game over; first one forces players to be serious, otherwise someone can be maimed or killed. Besides, if you want to have combat more than once during your game, you can't limit yourself to idigam, high-ranking spirits and unique Horrors. So, mid-hunt obstacles can be combat obstacles, and those obstacles need to be interesting/dangerous as well. Hence, this thread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451679#post1451679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is all to say, if you want to run the game you might be best served to check out the books. We've had people posting on the forum flabberghasted that their intricate mystery plot was solved with ease because they weren't aware of the capabilities of their Mage players, one of whom had enough Death arcana to just make the ghost, a point of the mystery, do whatever they wanted. All the advice offered in the thread won't matter if you have an entire pack face down something as mundane as a gang of humans with guns. They'd get hit with the hard side of the Down &amp; Dirty treatment if it even got to gauru, even if the same group could easily kill me in real life.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I've got this covered; in part due to my Mage experience as well. I think my antagonist is going to make players to fight for lives of their PCs. But I don't want boss fight to be the only fight of entire chronicle, and I want my other fights to be interesting/dangerous as well. Besides, some of advice should work pretty well for humans with guns; there just needs to be enough of those humans, with pretty heavy ordnance and some way to prevent pack from getting too close too fast.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T13:45:18-07:00","id":1451737,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451724#post1451724\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides, if you want to have combat more than once during your game, you can't limit yourself to idigam, high-ranking spirits and unique Horrors. So, mid-hunt obstacles can be combat obstacles, and those obstacles need to be interesting/dangerous as well. Hence, this thread.</div> </div> </div> </div>I want to add that if you do work up towards a huge and important combat scene as the climax of the game, you absolutely <b>need</b> to have combat scenes with pressure to establish themes and tone, to escalate the threat, and to prepare the players. The former two are just how to write a good story, which some might not be as interested in when it comes to RPGs, but the last one is extremely important. Without combat with actual stakes the players wouldn't have a good idea of how their characters actually works when combat comes on the table. Having multiple combat scenes is required to figure out tactics and synergies between characters, and for the GM to get an idea of what the PCs are capable of before they square up against the big bad.<br /> <br /> Besides, all CofD games revolve heavily around combat. Combat is the most elaborate and pervasive subsystem in the entire Storyteller system, the only subsystem that has its derived traits (Defense and Initiative, as well as the main reason for Health boxes) printed directly on the character sheets, and monopolises an entire category of Merits. You certainly can minimise or even ignore combat in a CofD game (including Werewolf), but unless that's stated before you start or join a game, you most certainly should expect combat to feature quite heavily.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T14:32:21-07:00","id":1451751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451557#post1451557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Our first sacred hunt in second edition had the target in a vehicle and shutting them down is very easy for werewolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>The same thing happened in the first session of both Werewolf 2e chronicles I've played in. We got into an interesting discussion about whether a werewolf in Urshul could use &quot;Leg wrack&quot; on a minivan.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451669#post1451669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there is big difference between &quot;celebrating the awesome&quot; and having protagonists which can't be meaningfully challenged without throwing a minor god at them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're seriously underselling the options you have as the Storyteller. If you're determined to give the Werewolves tough combat encounters you have far more options than they do. Build a Horror using the rules in the CofD Core Rulebook and give it the Unbreakable Dread Power. Make a Gangrel (or several) with Barbed Hands (Protean 4), a grapple pool of 15+, several dots of Resilience, and Unnatural Affinity (Werewolves). Equip a gang of minions with the weapons in Hurt Locker that have the Stun tag and abuse the hell out of that mechanic. Have them run afoul of the God Machine's angels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T02:22:05-07:00","id":1451820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451751#post1451751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're seriously underselling the options you have as the Storyteller. If you're determined to give the Werewolves tough combat encounters you have far more options than they do. Build a Horror using the rules in the CofD Core Rulebook and give it the Unbreakable Dread Power. Make a Gangrel (or several) with Barbed Hands (Protean 4), a grapple pool of 15+, several dots of Resilience, and Unnatural Affinity (Werewolves). Equip a gang of minions with the weapons in Hurt Locker that have the Stun tag and abuse the hell out of that mechanic. Have them run afoul of the God Machine's angels.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, each of them are not only from other books but also from books that aren't even WtF supplements. Not great arguments against a perceived flaw in the base game.<br /> That said, you do have some options for mid-tier enemies in high-Rank spirits, Hosts, Claimed, and enemy Uratha. The latter two have variants (Hive-Claimed and Empty Wolves) that are specifically created by idigam so extra appropriate if an idigam is the final threat of the game. Of course, no one really claimed there's a lack of mid-tier opponents. The above posts are replies to the claim that werewolf should be celebrated as mostly unstoppable god-killers, a point which would be ruined by introducing challenging mid-tier enemies.*<br /> But the main question of the thread is still, how do you balance such encounters? Enemy Uratha especially complicates things because they have the very same options as the PCs in whether to go Gauru and/or Kuruth or not.<br /> <br /> While second edition CofD is a step up from first edition, I think some modern design thoughts and ideas (celebrating the awesome, let players be powerful within the field of the game, playing literal god-killers) clashes with established themes (urban fantasy, the supernatural being subordinate and hidden from humanity, street level gameplay) and conventions (players being continuously challenged, threats escalating, giving the GM the tools to do either). I believe you absolutely can mix all of these together and get a coherent game, but it requires a balance that obviously people have trouble finding. I admit I have much less experience with first edition games but as far as I can tell, these problems weren't as common back then (not to say first edition games didn't have other problems) and that balancing problems in second edition (specifically, Werewolf combat and Mage mysteries) are shared both by 1e veterans (who understandably have preconceived expectations) and 2e newcomers (who experience the game at face value).<br /> But take what I'm saying with a grain of salt. Werewolf is not one of the games I've played all that much and I mostly base my opinion on reading and analysing the game as well as what others say about it.<br /> <br /> * Edited footnote: This is the ranger problem in D&amp;D 5e. Fighters are great at combat. Wizards are great at spellcasting. Thieves get their Sneak Attacks in various situations. But Rangers who are supposed to be good at exploration, instead skip the exploratory phase of the game (which is unfortunately really underdeveloped, but that's a whole different criticism). Meaning, if you pick a Ranger character to have something to do when exploring and travelling to new dungeons, you actually end up skipping having to do a lot of it which is just disappointing. Celebrating the overpowered nature of Uratha combat by not providing challenging combat is the exact same thing: You gloss over what the characters are focused on and supposed to be good at. The Gauru benefit of &quot;use Down &amp; Dirty against lesser foes&quot; is a literal representation of the problem of having such an approach to the game. (As well as being a stupid &quot;benefit&quot; anyway; Down &amp; Dirty generally favours the lesser opponent since it ignores all extra tricks the superior party has, as well as being a wholly OOC mechanism designed to streamline uninteresting combat brought into the game as an IC mechanism.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 06-12-2021, 05:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-12T08:06:59-07:00","id":1451855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451751#post1451751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're seriously underselling the options you have as the Storyteller. If you're determined to give the Werewolves tough combat encounters you have far more options than they do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, around half of those are variations on &quot;throw a minor god at them&quot;, to be honest. By itself, that's not a problem. Problem is incorporation this into the plot without it being non-sensical and stupid. For example, if your current Chronicle revolves around tracking down a brood of Azlu, you are pretty limited in what you can throw at them without breaking logic of fiction in half. A bunch of allied spider spirits? Easily. A cell of hunter tricked into thinking that werewolves are a problem? Easily. Coterie of Gangrel with very rare and specific character build for screwing up werewolves? Now that requires a lot of wriggling place to put that in. So yeah, Storyteller is pretty limited in options once he made specific story choices, in my opinion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1450550-how-to-make-keep-werewolf-combat-dangerous-and-interesting?p=1451751#post1451751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Equip a gang of minions with the weapons in Hurt Locker that have the Stun tag and abuse the hell out of that mechanic.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is valuable thought, though, and thank you for it. It's a reverse of action economy; instead of having more actions on side of NPC threat, you deny actions to PCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-07T01:30:40-07:00","id":1454433,"title":"","content":"Short answer:<br /> <br /> - When they shift to gauru, I make them win. They are the final predators and I want my players to feel it.<br /> - When they shift, they are in deep shit. Real life situation are non-stop vortex of mad violence. I throw at them ambushes or horde of enemies. I want them under pressure , shifting to other forms for tactics and for survival.<br /> - When they shift for a ritual fight, Gauru is always forbidden.<br /> - almost always bring with you a motherfucker with silver just in case","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800000\">-'' </span>We are the unsullied.<br /> We are the inheritors.<br /> We are the Pure <span style=\"color:#800000\">''-</span>","edited":{"userid":3773,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3773-helur","user":"Helur","date":". 07-07-2021, 04:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3773-helur","name":"Helur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3773_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"352"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}